  required/minLength/maxLength/email/pattern/range. [1mTrade-off:[0m slightly more ceremony to read than plain data; in
  exchange, a one-off rule a future form needs is a 3-line local function, with no change to the shared core.
  Complexity: moderate.

  [1mB. Plain-data schema (declarative descriptors)[0m — no functions in the schema at all:
  [34mconst[39m loginSchema = {
    [36musername[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36mminLength[39m: [32m3[39m },
    [36mpassword[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36mminLength[39m: [32m8[39m },
  };
  [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m interprets the known keys. [1mTrade-off:[0m the most readable, and it's JSON-serializable (a schema could later
  come from the server). But the rule vocabulary is closed — every new rule means editing the shared interpreter, and
  the first genuinely custom rule has nowhere to go. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mC. One composed validator function per field[0m — each field maps to a single function, built by composing primitives.
  [1mTrade-off:[0m maximum flexibility, minimum declarativeness; the schema stops being inspectable data, which rules out
  generating form UI or error summaries from it later. Complexity: moderate. I'd discard this one — it buys flexibility
  you didn't ask for at the cost of readability you will use daily.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m With several forms planned and their fields still unknown, the closed vocabulary in B is the thing
  most likely to bite — and the moment it does, you're editing shared code from inside a feature. A keeps the schema
  readable and inspectable while making extension local. If you'd rather have the simplest possible thing and accept
  editing the core when a new rule appears, B is a legitimate call.
